@var format = VDF 1.0
@var year = 2026

# The {{format}} Format

**VDF — Vacter Document File** is a document container that behaves like a
PDF from the outside and like a website from the inside. One small,
super-compressed `.vdf` file carries a Zolto source document, every image,
SVG and font it needs, and enough structure that any tool can verify its
integrity byte-for-byte.

![VDF banner](assets/banner.svg)

[note title="At a glance"]
- Starts with a text magic header `%VDF-1.0` — the same trick PDF uses
- Ends with a binary trailer holding offsets and CRC-32 checksums
- The payload is a standard ZIP — `unzip` and 7-Zip open it directly
- Everything is DEFLATE-compressed at maximum level
[/note]

## Why a new format?

PDF froze documents into print-era glyphs: fixed pages, binary blobs,
fonts nobody can reuse. VDF keeps the *file-feel* of PDF — one attachment,
one checksum, one predictable layout — but the payload is a living Zolto
document that reflows on phones, renders math natively, draws charts as
crisp SVG, and stays editable forever.

@card-group cols=3
@card title="PDF identity" icon="description"
A `%VDF-1.0` magic line, a `VDFT` binary trailer with CRC-32 checksums and a
`%%VDF-EOF` end marker, mirroring how PDF files frame themselves.
@/card
@card title="ZIP inside" icon="folder_zip"
The payload is a stock ZIP archive. Unzip it, 7-Zip it, script it with
Python `zipfile` — no lock-in, ever.
@/card
@card title="Zolto engine" icon="bolt"
The entry document is Zolto: Markdown plus math, charts, diagrams, vector
graphics and interactive widgets.
@/card
@/card-group

## The layout, byte by byte

A VDF file is three stacked sections. The 64-byte header makes the format
instantly recognizable. The ZIP payload holds a `mimetype` entry first
(uncompressed, like ODF), a `manifest.json` describing the document, the
entry `document.zl` and an `assets/` tree. The 64-byte trailer records the
ZIP offset and size plus CRC-32 checksums of the manifest and the entry
document, so corruption is detectable without unpacking anything.

## Compression you can feel

VDF compresses every entry with DEFLATE at maximum level. Text-heavy Zolto
sources shrink dramatically; SVGs (which are just text) shrink just as well.

@chart bar title="Typical compression by content type" subtitle="uncompressed vs packed, illustrative" legend=false
labels:
Zolto text
SVG art
Web font (woff2)
Manifest

data:
88
71
8
62
@/chart

## Native mathematics

Zolto renders mathematics to HTML and MathML without any external engine.
The Lorentz factor below is live markup, not an image:

@math label="eq:lorentz" name="Lorentz factor"
\gamma = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - v^2/c^2}}
@/math

As shown in @ref(eq:lorentz), energy grows without bound as $v \to c$. Inline
math like $E = \gamma m_0 c^2$ works anywhere in a paragraph.

## Diagrams as first-class citizens

@diagram sequence title="How a browser opens a VDF" id="vdf-flow"
node ext label="Browser" shape="actor"
node cli label="VDF viewer"
node zip label="ZIP payload" shape="cylinder"

ext ->> cli label="open report.vdf"
cli ->> zip label="seek to zip_offset (trailer)"
zip -->> cli label="document.zl + assets/"
cli -->> ext label="rendered HTML"
@/diagram

## Vector graphics without image files

The Zolto vector engine draws directly in the document — no asset round-trip:

@vector width=560 height=180 theme="dark"
artboard id="chip" x=10 y=10 w=540 h=160
rect x=0 y=0 w=540 h=160 radius=18 fill="#10142A" stroke="#2A3355" strokeWidth=1
circle cx=64 cy=80 r=30 fill="#5B3DF5" opacity=0.95
circle cx=64 cy=80 r=44 fill="none" stroke="#5B3DF5" strokeWidth=2 opacity=0.35
circle cx=64 cy=80 r=58 fill="none" stroke="#00C2D1" strokeWidth=2 opacity=0.25
text x=140 y=72 size=24 weight=700 fill="#FFFFFF"
one file · every asset
@endtext
text x=140 y=104 size=14 fill="#9AA6C4"
images, SVG, fonts — packed & checksummed
@endtext
@endartboard
@/vector

## Assets travel with the document

This file bundles three asset groups: the **banner SVG** you saw at the top,
the **logo** below, and two **subset web fonts** (regular + bold, ~21 KB
each) that a viewer can apply for pixel-perfect typography offline.

![The VDF logo](assets/logo.svg "VDF — Vacter Document File mark")

Term
: An archive-relative file stored under `assets/`, listed in `manifest.json`.

Font assets follow a simple convention: any `assets/fonts/*.woff2` is picked
up automatically by the official viewers and injected as a web font.

## Interactive documents

Because the payload is rendered by an engine, a VDF can ask questions:

@interactive {
@quiz "VDF knowledge check" {
@mcq "Which archive format lives inside a .vdf?" {
@correct "ZIP"
@choice "TAR"
@choice "7z"
@hint
It is the format every OS can already open.
@end
@explain
The payload is a standard ZIP archive — openable by unzip, 7-Zip and Python.
@end
}
@truefalse "The VDF trailer stores CRC-32 checksums." answer true
}
}
@/interactive

## A quick comparison

| | PDF | VDF |
|---|---|---|
| Magic header | `%PDF-1.7` | `%VDF-1.0` |
| Payload | object streams | ZIP + Zolto |
| Reflows on mobile | rarely | always |
| Math | embedded fonts | native markup |
| Interactivity | forms at best | full runtime |
| Openable without VDF tools | — | any ZIP tool |

## Summary

A `.vdf` is small enough to email, structured enough to verify, open enough
to unpack with anything, and rich enough to render an entire interactive
document. That is the whole idea.

Built with the Zolto engine — https://github.com/uxle/Zolto
